WEBVTT

00:00:00.00 --> 00:00:07.20
In the early twelfth century, Bianjing, the capital of the Northern Song, was the most prosperous city in the world.

00:00:07.20 --> 00:00:11.60
Its palaces were towering and magnificent, its markets thronged with people — a scene of perfect peace.

00:00:11.60 --> 00:00:20.00
But in the north, the Jin army raised its great banners and pressed closer to Bianjing, step by step. Warhorses galloped and flags streamed in the wind.

00:00:20.00 --> 00:00:25.80
The air was thick with tension. At last, the Jin army broke through Bianjing's tall city gate.

00:00:25.80 --> 00:00:30.80
Thick black smoke rose above the city walls, and the capital fell into panic.

00:00:30.80 --> 00:00:34.40
The great hall of the imperial palace stood empty, its scrolls and books scattered across the floor.

00:00:34.40 --> 00:00:38.00
The once-glorious court was left with nothing but deathly silence.

00:00:38.00 --> 00:00:44.00
Emperor Huizong, Emperor Qinzong, and many members of the imperial clan were captured and escorted north, as heavy snow fell.

00:00:44.00 --> 00:00:47.50
Their figures gradually faded away into the wind and snow.

00:00:47.50 --> 00:00:51.40
The Northern Song had fallen, but the young Zhao Gou stepped forward in the south.

00:00:51.40 --> 00:00:55.00
He founded the Southern Song, and history turned a new page.
